\section{The names}{

Fixed-effect components (\code{beta}, \code{betad}) carry the design-matrix
column names, so they agree with \code{\link[=fixef]{fixef()}} and \code{summary()}, and the
intercept is spelled \code{"(Intercept)"}. Covariance components
(\code{theta}, \code{thetaac}, \code{thetar}) have no design of their own and are
spelled \code{theta_1}, \code{theta_2}, ... - the same names \code{confint(parm =)}
reports. When you supply names BACK, the parentheses may be dropped:
\code{"Intercept"} and \code{"(Intercept)"} address the same coefficient.
}

\section{Two vocabularies, one model}{

This is the flat internal parameterization. \code{\link[=get_prior]{get_prior()}} describes
the same model in the structured \code{class}/\code{coef}/\code{group}/\code{dpar}/\code{nlpar}
vocabulary that \code{set_prior()} addresses, and \code{frm_simulate()} also
accepts a natural-scale spelling (\code{sigma}, \code{sd_g__Intercept}). Use
\code{get_prior()} to write priors and \code{par_template()} to write starting
values and box constraints.

brms has no counterpart for starting values: \code{brm(init =)} takes a
list of Stan program names and its own documentation calls it mainly
an internal testing facility. brms does not need one, because it
initializes at random in a bounded range and its priors carry
location information. \code{frm()} optimizes rather than samples, so it
evaluates the objective AT the starting values; this is the discovery
step that makes that survivable.
}

